WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:07.170
On July 26, 1971, the Apollo 15 space module
touched down on the moon and astronauts

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:10.110
emerged with a new vehicle, the Lunar Rover.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.120
They took it for a test drive.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:16.140
It handled well on the rough surface of the
moon so they could venture further from the

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.390
landing site and really explore.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:25.180
The lunar rover was no ordinary four
wheeler.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:30.850
Millions of dollars of research went into
this one horsepower wonder, which became

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:32.440
known as the moon buggy.

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:39.070
Today, craftsmen build replicas for space
museums and to use as movie props.

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:46.270
The team starts with the substantial
suspension system designed to smooth the ride

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:48.250
over the moon's rocky terrain.

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:52.810
Then they install a steering plate at the
front.

00:00:55.390 --> 00:01:00.010
They attach actuators, which are mechanisms
that assist in steering.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:06.380
They connect the actuator drive bars to the
steering plate.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:15.170
Together, the steering plate and actuators
give the lunar rover an incredibly tight

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.500
turning radius of just over three meters.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:25.830
They screw support brackets for the
actuators to the corners of the chassis.

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:31.720
Then they connect the actuators to them.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.640
They use steel and aluminum bolts and parts.

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:45.340
However, on the original moon buggy, many
parts were made of titanium because it

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:47.620
lightened the load carried into space.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.970
Titanium is expensive and unnecessary for
this replica.

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:55.120
The fenders are next.

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:58.000
They are extra large to protect astronauts
from.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:00.580
Lunar dust kicked up by the rover.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:08.420
They insert a locking mechanism in a slit at
the end of the drive shaft and slide the

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.190
wheel hub onto the shaft.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:19.190
They turned the hub to lock it in place, and
they're now ready for the wheels in the

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:21.150
atmospheric vacuum of the moon.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:27.020
Airfield rubber tires were out of the
question, so the original rover had wire mesh

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:29.520
tires to mimic the look.

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:32.150
They've wrapped a wire lattice around rubber
tires.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:39.750
The team secures the wheel to the hub with a
plastic and aluminum cap that's equipped with

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:40.920
locking pins.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:46.160
They give it a spin and install the other
three wheels.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:53.160
Next, a technician assembles the instrument
panel.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:58.110
He inserts various switches and gauges into
their slots.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.300
Including this system reset switch.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:08.070
It's used to bring the displays back to zero
prior to an expedition.

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:16.000
He closes a two piece collar around the
bottom of the switch.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.700
He locks it in place with a top cap.

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:34.460
He then flips the panel around and tightens
the nut on the back part of the switch.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:42.220
With the system reset switch now snug to the
board.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.070
The instrument panel is complete.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:47.440
He places it in the housing.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:56.380
He installs metal bars around the switches
to keep them from being accidentally tripped.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:04.900
He assembles the steering handle, which is a
t shaped joystick next to the instrument

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:10.810
panel. They now transfer the steering and
Instrument panel assembly to the vehicle.

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:17.920
They fastened the actuator switches to the
steering housing.

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:25.090
And then clip the wiring harness to the
floor of the lunar rover.

00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:32.380
They cover the bottom of the rover with flame
resistant material.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:41.460
They lower the electrical control panel and
battery into the chassis and connect them.

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:47.550
The original rover had two small batteries,
but the mechanics of this replica have had an

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:56.940
update. The production of this lunar rover
replica continues with the fabrication of a

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:58.020
brake disc.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.690
The technician carefully measures the
dimensions and draws them onto a sheet of

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:05.340
plastic covered aluminum.

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:14.910
He drills a large hole for the main drive
shaft and four screw holes.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:23.880
He then cuts out the disk following the
penciled lines.

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:31.620
He smooths the rough edges using a fine grit
abrasive belt.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:47.440
His hands off the plastic liner and a braids
the aluminum surface to a satiny sheen.

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:59.290
The brake disc is now ready for assembly to
the lunar rover.

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:07.250
He attaches it to the main drive shaft and
lowers the assembly into the vehicle.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:15.350
He bolts the disk to the chassis where it
will serve as a friction surface for the

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:18.050
brake pads which he installs next.

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:28.700
He connects it to a spring mechanism for
engaging the disc brake.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:36.210
He tests the drive shaft and disc brake and
confirms that they're both in working order.

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:46.270
He loops a roller chain around the sprocket
gear for the rear wheel drive and the teeth

00:06:46.270 --> 00:06:51.910
into mesh. He drives pins through the links
to join the ends, closing the Loop.

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:54.880
This drive chain is powered by an electric
motor.

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:59.420
It's another mechanical modification on the
original rover.

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:03.340
There was a small motor for every wheel and
no drive chain.

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:06.730
The lawn chair, like seats are next.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:13.200
They cover the shocks and battery at the
front with an aluminum hood.

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:22.840
They also install a cover over the mechanics
at the back.

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:26.950
It doubles as a rack for tools for
geological excavation.

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:33.590
They attach the umbrella antenna to the
rover.

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:37.460
It was used to beam pictures and data to
earth.

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:45.590
Next, an aluminum cylinder spins in a lathe
as cutting tools transform it into a mount

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:46.910
for a film camera.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:55.090
They also machine a bracket for a
communications antenna.

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:01.330
It's quite a transformation.

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:10.570
Using the bracket, a member of the crew now
erects the communications antenna.

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:13.630
He then installs the mount for the film
camera.

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:23.420
Next. Using a sheer press, he lops off a
piece of an aluminum sheet.

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:34.270
He switches to a hand tool and cuts out a
geometric shape with flaps.

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:47.180
He folds up the flaps using a brake press,
creating a box to cover the main battery.

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:54.280
After the seams have been welded.

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:58.450
He wraps the cover in Mylar, which is a
strong plastic film.

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:07.090
The wrap is an extra layer of protection
against wind borne particles known as micro

00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:13.720
meteoroids. They also wrap the camera and
other parts of the rover with Mylar.

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:24.760
This replica of the Apollo lunar rover is
complete and ready to expand the universe of

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:25.840
the curious.

